Add a GTK theme generator that follows the QtCurve settings

QtCurve ships an engine for GTK 2 and no theme for GTK 3 or GTK 4. A GTK
application on a Plasma desktop therefore does not match the Qt
applications. A sheet of baked colours cannot follow the settings that the
user saves.

The new program qtcurve-gtk-writer reads the settings through
qtcReadConfig() and the colour scheme through KColorScheme, and it writes
one sheet per dialect. It compiles common.cpp and config_file.cpp in and it
links qtcurve-utils. The shade ladders, the gradient stop table and the
option parser are therefore the ones of the Qt 6 style. A new option
reaches the theme with no second parser.

The build runs the program four times and installs the result, so a user
who never opened the dialogue still gets the look. GTK 4 loses the theme
when the dark sheet is absent, so the build writes all four sheets. A
check compares each shipped sheet with a fresh run at build time.

At run time the program writes qtcurve.css and the glyphs into the
configuration directory of the user. It adds one import line to gtk.css
with the idiom of kde-gtk-config, and it writes no byte when that line is
there. That keeps every foreign line, and a lost write costs 22 characters.

The style dialogue starts the program at the end of save(). A watch mode
covers a colour scheme change and a settings change from another program.
The program writes nothing while the GTK theme name is not QtCurve, and it
removes its files when that name leaves. GTK 3 keeps the colour of an SVG
file, so the GTK 3 sheet names no glyph and GTK 3 draws its own.

A write reaches an application at its next start. The Qt 6 style and
kde-gtk-config live under the same rule.

The Options struct at qt6/common/common.h:716-900 declares 175 named members. Five
are not user options: version, currentNonnativeMenubarApps, tickFont, menuTick and
fontTickWidth. The table therefore holds 170 fields. The sheet maps 49 of them, it
approximates 12 and it drops 109. A field counts as read when a line of gtk4/writer/
names it. The count adds round and customGradient, which reach the sheet through
qtcGetRadius() and qtcGetGradient().

The 49 fields that map: activeTabAppearance, animatedProgress, appearance,
bgndAppearance, bgndGrad, boldProgress, borderInactiveTab, borderMenuitems,
borderProgress, borderSbarGroove, borderSelection, borderTab, contrast, crButton,
customGradient, customMenubarsColor, customProgressColor, customShades, darkerBorders,
forceAlternateLvCols, grooveAppearance, gtkScrollViews, highlightFactor, highlightTab,
lighterPopupMenuBgnd, lvAppearance, lvButton, menuBgndAppearance, menuBgndGrad,
menubarAppearance, menuitemAppearance, popupBorder, progressAppearance, progressColor,
progressGrooveAppearance, progressGrooveColor, round, sbarBgndAppearance,
selectionAppearance, shadeMenubars, shading, sliderAppearance, sliderFill, sliderWidth,
sunkenAppearance, tabAppearance, tabBgnd, toolbarAppearance, tooltipAppearance.

The 12 fields that the sheet approximates, with the loss:

* focus, coloredMouseOver and buttonEffect hold five, four and three values, and the
  sheet draws one ring, one border colour and one shadow.
* stripedProgress holds three values, and the sheet draws one repeating gradient.
* groupBox and toolbarBorders reach the sheet as the none case alone.
* square reaches it through two bits, SQUARE_TOOLTIPS and SQUARE_POPUP_MENUS, and
  gbLabel through the bold bit.
* customAlphas reaches it through ALPHA_ETCH_DARK alone.
* titlebarAppearance paints the headerbar node, which is not the window decoration.
* etchEntry gates the fade of the lower border of an entry, of a spin button and of a
  list view. The sheet draws no etch ring around those nodes.
* sliderStyle holds five values and reaches the sheet through the tick offset alone,
  and the sheet draws one handle shape.

The 109 fields that the sheet drops fall into eight groups. Each group names the CSS
limit that blocks it, so no option leaves the scope without a reason.

1. The window decoration, 12 fields. GTK 4 has no decoration node, and the shadow of a
   Wayland toplevel belongs to the compositor. dwtAppearance, dwtSettings,
   inactiveTitlebarAppearance, shadowSize, titlebarAlignment, titlebarButtonAppearance,
   titlebarButtonColors, titlebarButtons, titlebarEffect, titlebarIcon, windowBorder,
   windowDrag.
2. The lists of application names, 11 fields. CSS cannot read the name of a process.
   menubarApps, noBgndGradientApps, noBgndImageApps, noBgndOpacityApps,
   noMenuBgndOpacityApps, noMenuStripeApps, nonnativeMenubarApps, statusbarApps,
   useQtFileDialogApps, windowDragBlackList, windowDragWhiteList.
3. A setting of the toolkit and not of the sheet, 18 fields. buttonStyleMenuSections,
   drawStatusBarFrames, embolden, gtkButtonOrder, gtkComboMenus, hideShortcutUnderline,
   mapKdeIcons, menuCloseDelay, menuDelay, menuIcons, menubarHiding, onlyTicksInMenu,
   passwordChar, reorderGtkButtons, statusbarHiding, stdBtnSizes, stdSidebarButtons,
   toolbarTabs.
4. A node, a layer or a shape that the sheet does not draw, 31 fields. coloredTbarMo,
   comboSplitter, customMenuStripeColor, defBtnIndicator, doubleGtkComboArrow,
   expanderHighlight, fadeLines, fillProgress, fillSlider, flatSbarButtons,
   glowProgress, handles, highlightScrollViews, lvLines, menuStripe,
   menuStripeAppearance, scrollbarType, shadePopupMenu, sliderThumbs, splitters,
   stripedSbar, tbarBtnAppearance, tbarBtnEffect, tbarBtns, thinSbarGroove,
   toolbarSeparators, unifyCombo, unifySpin, unifySpinBtns, vArrows, xCheck.
5. A state that CSS cannot reach, or a property that the emitter does not write, 13
   fields. centerTabText, colorMenubarMouseOver, colorSelTab, colorSliderMouseOver,
   crHighlight, invertBotTab, menubarMouseOver, roundAllTabs, roundMbTopOnly,
   shadeMenubarOnlyWhenActive, splitterHighlight, tabMouseOver, useHighlightForMenu.
6. A size branch, 4 fields. CAN_EXTRA_ROUND at qt6/common/common.cpp:192 needs a
   measured width and a measured height, and CSS has no branch on a size. crSize,
   gbFactor, smallRadio, thin.
7. The opacity and the background images, 7 fields. A theme cannot make a window
   translucent, and the writer copies no pixmap. bgndImage, bgndOpacity, bgndPixmap,
   dlgOpacity, menuBgndImage, menuBgndOpacity, menuBgndPixmap.
8. A colour of a node that group 4 already drops, 13 fields. comboBtn, crColor,
   customCheckRadioColor, customComboBtnColor, customCrBgndColor,
   customMenuNormTextColor, customMenuSelTextColor, customMenuTextColor,
   customSlidersColor, customSortedLvColor, shadeCheckRadio, shadeSliders, sortedLv.

The design panel scored three candidates against the six criteria of plan section 3.2.
The static theme won on points, with 46 of 60, against 44 for the split theme and 41
for the runtime generator. The user then set a binding constraint: the GTK theme must
follow the QtCurve settings of the user. A GTK application then looks fluent beside the
Qt applications. The constraint outranks the totals, and the runtime generator became
the base.

The split theme gave this work the cooperation mechanics for every file that the daemon
owns. It also gave the neutral value idiom, the gate for name completeness and the gate
for dead selectors. The static theme gave the fallback that ships in the package, so the
theme name works for a user who never saved. Both outputs come from one emitter, and a
byte compare guards against drift. The header table names every option that the theme
drops, with the CSS limit that blocks it.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree. The push puts
that branch on the fork wyn/qtcurve. The merge request on system/qtcurve
follows that push.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

